  "notes": "The London europop room that industrialised pitch-processed dance-pop and Latin-pop crossover: 122–133 straight, four-on-the-floor with claps on 2&4, and ballads at 82–100 which are named here rather than encoded because they carry the other backbeat class. The whole drum block is quoted: \"kick on all four\" (density 4 on the four anchors with `syncopation` at 0.05), \"snare and clap layered on 2&4\", \"**open hat on every off-8th**\" (a 0.9 probability across all four \"&\"s at two to four per bar — the busiest open hat in this section), and \"16th shaker\" plus \"a filtered-noise riser **plus one bar of kick-only** into each chorus\", which is the shaker/riser perc block and a very high `dropOutBeats`. Harmony spans both wings they are documented for: the 1998 flagship's eight-chord cycle I–V–iii–IV–I–vi–iii–vii, and the ballads' i–VI–III–VII with a IV–V–I chorus lift — `maxChords` 8 to hold the long one. The bass is \"8th-note synth bass on roots, ducked under the kick, dropping out for the vocal-hook bars\". Their signature is the voice treated as a synth — Auto-Tune at an extreme retune speed so portamento disappears — which a note generator can only say as a staccato articulation with a high chord-tone bias. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and the melody numbers; the DAW, synths, recorders, mics, tempo, key, progression and vocal method are all sourced, the values are not.",
